 unit treatment value assumption  so basically this means that the  treatment of one individual does not in  turn change the potential outcomes of  other individuals  and  this is not always the case um indeed  sometimes especially if we're thinking  of  a market you know perhaps um  how other people are perceived and in  turn the  hiring decisions of other people might  affect whether there's still places  available for you to be  hired for that same role  and there's also  consistency so there needs to be only  one version of each treatment  so this can also be limiting and say you  know there might be  we might not only have gender  discrimination as a binary so perhaps  there are different layers if someone is  non-binary or someone is trans and so  we would want to very explicitly model  every relevant type of  treatment  there's also positivity so at each level  of pre-treatment variables the  probability of  receiving any treatment must be strictly  positive  and then finally we have conditional  exchange abilities so we don't have any  unobserved confounders that affect both  the treatment assignment and the  potential outcome  once we condition on the pre-treatment  variables and again this  depending on the data you have available  in the scenario can be quite a strong  assumption  um so in terms of how we actually try to  assess  unfairness in this context so of course  this is quite easy if we actually had  access to both potential outcomes we  could just very quickly  calculate the difference for every  individual and  take an average and then we would have a  nice estimate  but in practice we only ever observe one  potential outcome for each individual so  in terms of imputing the other potential  outcome  there's many different methods that can  be used in this case what we do is we  choose one of the groups as a baseline  group so in this case perhaps the men  are treated as a baseline and then we  train a model for the probability of  being hired conditional on certain  pre-treatment attributes if you are  treated the same way as  the male individuals  and then we can apply this to the female  individuals to impute  what their hiring decision would have  been if they were treated in the same  way as the male individuals  and so in this way  we can try to  start answering that question of  but for an individual's gender would  they have been hired and in this way  hopefully we can  develop inference that also maps on to  how people think about discrimination in  the legal context  um so in our paper we also go through  and um develop some causal equivalence  of many of the common statistical  fairness notions like parity calibration  positive predictive parity and equalized  odds um i won't have time today to run  through all of these  but instead i'll illustrate this a  little bit in uh um  in a numerical way in the numerical  results we derived from  a synthetic example using these sorts of  metrics  so basically we synthesized some data  here where we have um several different  sources of disparity that we want to  distinguish between using causal versus  statistical notions of fairness  and in the synthetic  hiring scenario we have alpha which is  the level of disparity in the job  qualifications between the two different  genders  for the sake of this toy example we just  have two groups in terms of gender  and then we have beta which is the level  of disparity in the interview score for  the level of job qualification and so  this is one form of bias this is bias in  the interview process and then we have  gamma which is the level of  discrimination in the job decision  conditional on the job qualification and  interview score so this is um yet  another form of bias this is the bias  that occurs after  the interview process  and we also consider in this two  different starting points for the  unfairness evaluation before the  interview and after the interview in  order to distinguish between beta and  gamma and so these are the  numerical results we get from these  experiments and so basically on the  x-axis we have beta which is the  discrimination in the interview score  and then on the y-axis we have the  fairness evaluation that we measure and  we have three different cases here for  different levels of alpha so this is a  baseline where there is no difference in  qualifications across gender  and then these are where one of the  genders have higher qualifications  and first  i want to point out that so for causal  post which is the green  line it's flat throughout which makes  sense because basically that's when we  evaluate bias after  the interview process and since beta is  biased in the interview process  that should be flat throughout so it's  flat but non-zero because it's  reflecting gamma which is the bias  outside of the interview process  for causal pre and statistical though  what we would ideally like to see  is that  the bias should increase with beta since  for those two metrics we're trying to  measure  both the bias from beta and also from  gamma  and the bias should be non-negative  ideally  and so  when we look at the scenario where there  are no disparities in qualifications  between the different genders we see  similar trends  for causal pre and statistical so we  can't compare apples and apples apples  to apples the actual um  the actual axes exactly so you should um  so we don't need to worry too much about  the exact slopes  or the exact intersection with the  y-axis but we can see the trends are the  same in terms of starting  at  a fairly low level of bias that's  positive and then going up with beta  this changes however and the two types  of metrics diverge when we have  differing baselines between the  different demographic groups  and so in the scenario where females  have higher qualifications than males  in this case the causal  pre  still has quite similar trends to what  we saw  before in that we have positive  levels of fairness violation for all of  these and it increases with beta  for the statistical notion however now  the bias is measured as being negative  because  since the bias is against females but in  this scenario females have higher  qualifications the fact that the  statistical notion of fairness does not  separate between these differing sources  of bias  leads to a very different  a very different interpretation of  whether the model is actually biased  in contrast if we have at baseline that  the males have higher qualifications  than the females in this scenario the  statistical notion of fairness  significantly overestimates on the level  of unfairness in comparison to the  causal pre  and so the major takeaways  from this work are first that  causal analysis at different points can  help identify  more precisely specific sources of  disparities and distinguish between them  parity-based causal and statistical  fairness notions converge when we don't  have disparities in the baseline  attributes  but they  do diverge when it comes to  differing baselines  and in those contexts the statistical  notions of fairness either over or  underestimate the level of unfairness  as a result statistical fairness is not  capable of distinguishing unfairness at  each of these different time points  and ultimately our framework also  underscores the need for data sets that  span this decision-making pipeline so  in our synthetic example we were able to  very specifically distinguish  what were pre-treatment variables versus  poached treatment and separate out the  interview score as well  in practice that can often be difficult  to distinguish especially when gender  or whatever your sensitive attribute is  is perceived at various points in the  decision making process and so having  data that can help you delineate that  can be quite critical for distinguishing  whether the bias is due to one part of  the process versus another part um so  that's the first paper and i see there's  um a question in the chat oh ten minutes  left yep thanks okay i will speed things  up with the second presentation  um okay so let me see yes i'll go  through this very quickly then um so  this is human-centric visual diversity  auditing um this is joint work with  drone andrews and shamik joniak  who are also  on the sony ai team and shimak was our  intern over the summer  so basically um some to set some context  for this work um so when we talk about  fairness in the tabular data context  we're often worried about these  discriminatory trends in our data  in the computer vision context typically  the primary problem driving unfairness  is  lack of diversity in our data sets and  this is just one example illustrating  this so on the left hand side we have  the original image in the middle we have  a down sampled image and on the right  hand side we  have applied a model to reconstruct the  face and we can see that the right hand  images have ended up looking much more  caucasian and much more male in  comparison to the left-hand images and  this is due to biases  in the  um  in the model that in the face  restoration model which has seen far  more images of males and caucasian  individuals than of um female or darker  skinned individuals  and so um even though diversity in human  image data sets is a very critical  concern for fairness it's quite  difficult in practice to actually  achieve diversity so the canonical way  that people approach this at the moment  is  to discretize certain demographic  categories so usually people think of  gender race and age  and then to try to guarantee parity  across these different groups so equal  number of images in each of these groups  and this has a number of shortcomings so  first off  this does not necessarily capture all of  the visual diversity we would be  interested in so for example on the  right hand side we see images that all  fall under this caucasian female 20 to  39 bucket but it's a very homogeneous  looking set of images  so  using these categories doesn't  necessarily capture all the diversity  we're interested in  and then in addition to that oftentimes  in research people assume that we have  access to labeled information about  people's demographics and this is  typically not actually true in practice  and so often this involves  relying on human annotators to create  these labels and of course that  then in turn adds additional layers of  bias  also makes it quite difficult for people  who might not  fit very neatly within your demographic  taxonomy to be seen  so for example if you discretize  gender into just male and female on  people who are non-binary will not um be  necessarily featured appropriately or  people who are multi-ethnic might not be  captured  with a simple race characterization  um so instead what we do in this paper  is we try to train a model to actually  um learn  a facial embedding space of what  dimensions people think are relevant for  facial similarity and then we use this  model to be able to generate diversity  scores for human image data sets  and so the  intuition behind how we do this is so we  have these triplets of human face images  and we ask people to choose to odd one  out so obviously which  person looks the least similar to the  other two  and by doing this we are implicitly  learning the different dimensions that  people think are relevant for judging  facial similarity so for example  for the first triplet you can imagine  that maybe people would think the middle  image is the odd one out because of a  hair color difference versus in the  second  triplet people might pick the third  image  due to differences in gender and age  compared to the other two  and so  through all of these triplet judgments  we're able to identify these relevant  dimensions  and so the basic idea is that  our model is learning these face  embeddings that hopefully should capture  the various dimensions that people are  implicitly using and in this way we're  not requiring folks to explicitly label  the images but instead to reflect upon  just visual similarity  and in order to actually test whether  the dimensions our model learns are  actually meaningful  question from the audience to mind uh  oh yeah yeah  hi alice and  in this  method that you have for  evaluating diversity  it seems like it's circular because you  need the diversity in the triplets that  you are presenting  so  if you don't have diversity enough  diversity in the triplets of images that  you show then you will never be able to  get diversity in the outcome  so it's somehow you you are  using as input the type of  outcome that you would like to get i  mean it's it looks a bit circular  so some diversity is required in the  training data set for this but one  advantage of our approach is you can the  model can't extrapolate beyond the  diversity that it has seen um in that it  basically learns these dimensions that  are relevant for diversity but you could  have you know scores of diversity that  are higher than what was actually seen  in the training data set  way of formally stating it  um in the in the paper  we do  um  but um  but yeah if all triplets will be  white  people between the age of 20 and 30  then you will never  figure out diversity with respect to  older people or black people because  they haven't been shown in the triplets  yeah so you need every relevant  type of feature to be represented  in the data set but you don't  necessarily need the whole spectrum so  for example if you have zero diversity  at all in skin tone in the training set  then it of course cannot learn that skin  tone is relevant but if you have  some degree of diversity so let's say  you have fitzpatrick scale  two to four but you don't have one to  six it can still learn that skin tone is  relevant and then you know that if you  do later on have  images that have a wider range of skin  tone that that is a more diverse set  um but  yeah you do need to have at least some  degree of diversity  in that dimension in the training set  um so yeah um  so just to quickly run through um this  part over here so basically um so after  we trained this model our goal was to  try to see whether these dimensions are  meaningful or human interpretable and so  for each dimension what we did was  we arranged them in descending order and  then first we asked human participants  to label these dimensions so basically  to have one to three labels of how they  would characterize this dimension  um and then afterward we um created word  clouds in terms of what sorts of um  labels people tend to ascribe to them  and so here you can see um you know some  some of the categories that emerged did  seem quite similar to what people often  think of in terms of demographic  categories so you have like male  female  black caucasian east asian south asian  and elderly  but we also did find certain dimensions  that were not what we typically see in a  lot of you know demographic labels and  so we have dimensions around people's  facial shapes people's facial  expressions facial hair  and also whether their hair was dyed or  not so there's also these other implicit  dimensions of diversity that people are  using for judging whether faces are  similar that you know might not be  included otherwise if we are just  labeling um the dimensions  via demographics  and the second approach we took was to  again for each dimension have um have  images  um ordered in descending order and then  to introduce a novel image and ask human  participants to place this novel image  somewhere along  the spectrum  and then to compare that to where our  model would place it  and we found that there was a pretty  high correlation between where humans  would place it versus where the model  would place it which  further affirmed to us that there was  something on that our model was learning  that it maps onto how humans would judge  similarity and that these dimensions  were capturing something that is human  interpretable  and then finally the last thing we  looked at here was of course one of the  original motivations for this is we  wanted to  sidestep some issues of annotator bias  that come with explicitly labeling  demographics but we were still worried  about the extent to which there might be  annotator bias so we also checked for  that here and we did find that if we  used the annotator specific mask and  included that in our model it did  improve our model's performance and so  that does imply that there is  information in the annotator specific  mask  and this also does imply that different  people  see different dimensions as being of  different levels of importance for  judging facial similarity  and then finally we checked to see  whether there were systematic  differences here and we did find that  their worst  were differences um in terms of  how people weighed different dimensions  depending on if they were male or female  european or asian and also depending on  their age  so this further affirms that it's quite  important to have a very diverse set of  annotators and also to be quite aware of  potential annotator biases because there  isn't necessarily you know one way of  determining whether um different faces  look more similar or different  so  sorry for that very well whirlwind  set of two frozen stations but hopefully  that  hopefully that gave you a bit of a  flavor for both of these papers and  happy to answer any questions if we have  time  terrific um thank you so much alice i  don't see questions in chat yet and ben  is going to help us if there are  questions in the room  i have a question here from jordan  hi so i'm curious in the end does this  allow you to do better on the task you  showed us in the beginning the down  sample reconstruct task if for instance  you sort of uh  look at your data and try to make it  more equidistributed in this embedding  that you find do you do better on that  task  um ye